<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:lang="en" 
  xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron">
  
  <!-- Kate and Katia to input information about funders. I guess
  if there is more than one funder, than create a new funder tag
  for each one?-->
  <teiHeader>
    <fileDesc>
      <titleStmt>
        <title>Dostoevschema, the ODD for Computational Dostoevsky</title>
        <funder>Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council</funder>
        <author xml:id="KH">Dr. Kate Holland</author>
        <author xml:id="KB">Dr. Katherine Bowers</author>
        <author xml:id="BB">Dr. Braxton Boyer</author>
        <author xml:id="JT">Joey Takeda</author>
      </titleStmt>
      
      <!-- Kate and Katia to update information in the `<publicationStmt>`.
        Note that this is the place just for information about the ODD!-->
      <publicationStmt xml:id="dd-pubStmt">
        <publisher>Computational Dostoevsky</publisher>
        <pubPlace>Vancouver, British Columbia</pubPlace>
        <address>
          <addrLine>Department of Central, Eastern and Northern European Studies</addrLine>
          <addrLine>University of British Columbia</addrLine>
        </address>
        <availability>
          <licence target="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal</licence>
        </availability>
      </publicationStmt>
      <sourceDesc>
        <p>Born digital, created by the Computational Dostoevsky team with the help of Joey Takeda.</p>
      </sourceDesc>
    </fileDesc>
    <revisionDesc>
      <listChange>
        <change when="2026-08-27" who="#BB">Re-organizing the ODD to make it easier to read and update. 
          Note that myself and Joey Takeda first began this ODD in April 2026 but did not include commentary 
          about changes at that time.</change>
      </listChange>
    </revisionDesc>
  </teiHeader>
  
  <!-- Fill in project documentation here within the `<body>` tag. -->
  <text>
    <body>
      
      <!-- Braxton to fill in the introduction. Use as many
      paragraphs as needed.-->
      <div xml:id="introduction">
        <head>Introduction</head>
        <p>This is the technical documentation and guidelines for the Computational Dostoevsky project...</p>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Kate and Katia to write the history of the project below.
        Can really include whatever you want!  Use as many paragraphs as needed.-->
      <div xml:id="projectHistory">
        <head>History of the Project</head>
        <p>This project began as "Digital Dostoevsky" based at the University of Toronto in 2019, led by PI Kate Holland and co-PI Katherine Bowers (UBC). It aimed to create a corpus of 7 novels by Fedor Dostoevskii, encode them using TEI-XML, and then use a variety of computational text analysis methods to examine the encoded corpus. "Digital Dostoevsky" was funded by an Insight Grant from the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada. From 2019-2025, the project employed 10 undergraduate and graduate student Research Assistants. Library and university staff provided significant advising and support, including Eka Grgurić (UBC), Quinn Daedal (Stanford), Andy Janco (Princeton), Ksenya Kiebuzinski (Toronto), and Simon Wiles (Stanford).</p>
        <p>The texts used for encoding were extracted from the Soviet Academy of Sciences complete collected works of Fedor Dostoevskii, published from 1972-1990. Graduate RAs Braxton Boyer and Lena Vasileva digitized the texts, removed any frontmatter and notes, and checked the plain text files for errors. Holland, Bowers, Boyer, and Vasileva encoded the first novel, Dvoinik (The Double), after which the team developed and led a research workshop for undergraduate students funded by the Jackman Humanities Institute Scholars in Residence program. This May 2022 workshop trained six University of Toronto undergraduate students in TEI-XML and supported their encoding work on the project. The following students were a part of this workshop: Anastasiya Gordiychuk, Dmytro Ishchenko, Nadia Ivanova, Elijah Sciborowski, Veronika Sizova, and Eden Zorne. Their work was expedited by a tagger tool created by Wiles. His tagger, designed specifically for this project, adds blank XML tags around elements including character names, locations, and speech. These elements could then be filled in manually by students. Following the workshop, several students continued on as project RAs (Ishchenko, Ivanova, Sciborowski, Sizova, and Zorne). They were joined by two other University of Toronto graduate students, Liza Shershneva and Sydney Vermeersch. By the fall of 2024, the corpus had been completely encoded with a first pass. 2024-25 was spent checking and editing the encoding.</p>
        <p>In 2025, PI Bowers and co-PI Holland received a new SSHRC Insight Grant for the second phase of the project, "Computational Dostoevsky." This grant moved the project from the University of Toronto to the University of British Columbia. It allowed Bowers to hire a postdoctoral research fellow to support data management for the project. Dr Braxton Boyer joined Bowers in this role at UBC in February 2026. "Computational Dostoevsky" also has received significant advising and support from library and university staff, including Eka Grgurić (UBC) and Joey Takeda (Simon Fraser). Takeda's participation was made possible by a memorandum of understanding between UBC Library and the SFU Digital Humanities Innovation Lab; this memorandum was facilitated by the Digital Scholarship in Arts program at UBC directed by Christine D'Onofrio. "Computational Dostoevsky" aims to publish the finalized TEI edition of 7 Dostoevskii novels and also support Bowers and Holland's authorship of a monograph.</p>
        <p>Both "Digital Dostoevsky" and "Computational Dostoevsky" have been enhanced by training team members undertook through the UBC Library's Research Commons and the Digital Humanities Summer Institute over the years. The team wishes to thank its instructors including Elisa Beshero-Bondar, Jacek Bankowski, David Birnbaum, Marie-Hélène Burle, Joanna Byszuk, Constance Crompton, Randa El-Khatib, Grace Fishbein, Emily Murphy, Jessica Otis, Lydia Vermeyden, David Wrisley, and Lee Zickel. The project has also been grateful for the advice and support of the members of the Data-Sitters Club over the years: Lee Skallerup Bessette, Maria Sachiko Cecire, Quinn Daedal, Anouk Lang, and Roopika Risam.</p>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Braxton to write first draft of encoding practices.
      Kate and Katia to add/edit after. Use as many
      paragraphs as needed.-->
      <div xml:id="encodingPractices">
        <head>Encoding Practices</head>
          <p>Text here...</p>
      </div>
      
    </body>
    <back>
      
      <!-- Braxton to write the schema specification below -->
      <div xml:id="schema">
        <head>Schema Specification</head>
        <!--NOTE (JT, 2026-06-29): There is currently a bug with
          TEI >= 4.10.0 where a schematron abstract pattern is not
          included and thus breaks the output schema, which is why
          we specify 4.9.0 as the base schema for now. -->
      <schemaSpec ident="dostoevschema" start="TEI" prefix="tei_" targetLang="en" docLang="en"
        source="tei:4.9.0">
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: tei
            
            Include everything from the TEI module,
            including attribute classes, model classes,
            datatypes, and macros
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="tei"/>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: core
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="core"
          include="addrLine address author biblStruct date editor emph
          foreign head hi imprint item l lb lg list milestone
          monogr name note p ptr pubPlace publisher q quote resp
          respStmt rs said title"/>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: header
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="header"
          include="biblFull change creation fileDesc funder langUsage
          language principal profileDesc publicationStmt revisionDesc
          sourceDesc teiHeader titleStmt"/>
        
        <elementSpec ident="revisionDesc" module="header" mode="change">
          <content>
            <elementRef key="change" minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
          </content>
        </elementSpec>
        
        
        <elementSpec ident="change" module="header" mode="change">
          <classes mode="change">
            <memberOf key="att.global" mode="delete"/>
            <memberOf key="att.docStatus" mode="delete"/>
            <memberOf key="att.typed" mode="delete"/>
          </classes>
          <constraintSpec mode="add" scheme="schematron" ident="change.alwaysChronological">
            <desc>Change elements in revisionDesc should always follow reverse chronological order.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:change[following-sibling::tei:change]">
                <sch:let name="when" value="xs:date(@when)"/>
                <sch:let name="nextWhen" 
                  value="xs:date(following-sibling::tei:change[1]/@when)"/>
                <sch:assert test="not($when lt $nextWhen)">ERROR: All change elements should be listed in chronological order.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="target" mode="delete"/>
            <attDef ident="who" usage="req" mode="change"/>
            <attDef ident="when" usage="req" mode="change"/>
            <attDef ident="period" mode="delete"/>
            <attDef ident="when-custom" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: textstructure
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="textstructure"
          include="TEI body div epigraph front text"/>
        
        <elementSpec ident="div" module="textstructure" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec mode="add" scheme="schematron" ident="div.nestTypesProperly">
            <desc>Constrain div/@type to enforce the proper hierarchy (parts, then chapters, then sections).</desc>
            <constraint>
              
              <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type][ancestor::tei:div[@type]]">
                <sch:let name="allTypes" value="('part','chapter','section')"/>
                <sch:let name="currType" value="@type"/>
                <sch:let name="ancestorDivType"
                  value="ancestor::tei:div[@type][1]/@type"/>
                <sch:let name="currIdx" 
                  value="index-of($allTypes, $currType)"/>
                <sch:let name="ancestorIdx" 
                  value="index-of($allTypes, $ancestorDivType)"/>
                <sch:assert test="$ancestorIdx lt $currIdx">ERROR: Types of divisions must nest properly (<sch:value-of select="string-join($allTypes,', ')"/>).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
              <valList mode="add" type="closed">
                <valItem ident="part" mode="add">
                  <gloss>part</gloss>
                  <desc>The larger section, usually containing chapters (formerly <gi>div1</gi>).</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="chapter" mode="add">
                  <gloss>chapter</gloss>
                  <desc>The chapter, usually containing sections (formerly <gi>div2</gi>)</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="section" mode="add">
                  <gloss>section, formerly <gi>div3</gi></gloss>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: linking
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="linking" include="anchor seg standOff timeline when"/>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: namesdates
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="namesdates"
          include="addName forename listObject listPerson listPlace
          object objectIdentifier objectName orgName persName
          person personGrp place placeName roleName state surname"/>
        
        <elementSpec ident="person" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <content>
            <sequence>
              <elementRef key="persName" minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
              <elementRef key="note" minOccurs="0"/>
            </sequence>
          </content>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="xml:id" usage="req" mode="change"/>
            <!--TODO: Update usage to req once all errors/
              values have been resolved-->
            <attDef ident="sex" usage="rec" mode="change"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        
        <!-- Constrains `<addName>` to a closed list of `@type` values.-->
        <elementSpec ident="addName" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change">
              <valList mode="replace" type="closed">
                <valItem ident="patronymic">
                  <gloss>patronymic</gloss>
                  <desc>a person's patronymic, for example "Fyodorovich."</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="diminutive">
                  <gloss>diminutive</gloss>
                  <desc>the diminutive version of a character's name, for example "Mitya" for "Dmitry."</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="nickname">
                  <gloss>nickname</gloss>
                  <desc>a name given to a character by another character</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="epithet">
                  <gloss>epithet</gloss>
                  <desc>a name given to a character by the narrator, for example "our hero" for Golyadkin in The Double</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="pseudonym">
                  <gloss>pseudonym</gloss>
                  <desc>a character's or historical figure's pseudonym, for example "Voltaire."</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
          <exemplum>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
              Пред ним опять стояла <persName ref="#vps"><forename>Варвара</forename> <addName type="patronymic">Петровна</addName></persName>
            </egXML>
          </exemplum>
          <remarks>
            <p>In this project, <gi>addName</gi> is used only for references to names in the text; we do not include it within the structured data of a <gi>person</gi> element.</p>
          </remarks>
        </elementSpec>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: spoken
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="spoken" include="shift writing"/>
        
        
        <!--*************************************
            MODULE: verse
            *************************************-->
        <moduleRef key="verse" include="att.metrical"/>
        
      </schemaSpec>
      </div>
    </back>
  </text>
</TEI>